WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.780
This is slime.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.220
You might have expected that from the title
of the video.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.720
Even if you didn't, you might have just looked
at it and thought, "Yeah, yeah that looks

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.200
like slime."

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.760
Slime has had a bit of a cultural journey
in the last few decades.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.550
Some of us grew up watching green slime getting
dumped on the heads of game show contestants.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:38.070
And there's also been a bit of a slime renaissance
recently, with entire YouTube channels and

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:45.100
Instagram accounts dedicated to teaching people
how to make their own gooey, sticky blobs

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.100
at home with chemistry and a few simple ingredients.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.880
Of course, the most important step in any
of these slime projects is saying, "It's aliiiiiiiive,"

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.420
while you watch it spread, even when you know
that, of course, it’s not actually alive.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:06.280
The microcosmos, on the other hand, got to
its experiments long before we did, giving

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:12.970
us slime molds: a slime that not only lives, it learns.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:19.690
a slime that fuses microbes together to create
a living thing that grows into something we

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.800
can observe with our own eyes.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:29.000
You may have seen slime molds before, their
bodies sometimes laden with spores as they

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:31.910
grow on plants and decaying wood.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:37.650
They look kind of like fungi, and for that
reason, that is how they were initially classified.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:43.840
Except that one of the defining characteristics
of fungi is their inability to absorb and

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.360
digest their food internally.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.590
Slime molds, however, are plenty capable of
that.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:54.520
They do phagocytosis all the time.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.500
Where we see decaying matter, slime molds
detect a cornucopia of microbes.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:11.250
Plus, fungi do not move, and slime molds--in
their own unsettling way, do.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:17.750
We know now that slime molds are eukaryotes,
and they’re made up of amoeboid organisms.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:23.769
But they don’t fit neatly into any of our
definitions of plant, animal, or fungus, leaving

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:30.760
them in the more ambiguous Protista kingdom
and clustered under the label Mycetozoa.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.980
However, that label is a, get this, polyphyletic
grouping, and that means that the organisms

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:43.379
we’ve lumped together as slime molds aren’t
necessarily all related.

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:49.690
They’ve just found their way, over the course
of evolution, into a set of similarities that

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:56.879
is both convenient and misleading in our attempts
to categorize the natural world.

00:02:56.879 --> 00:02:59.950
There are three major types of slime molds.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.439
There are the Protostelids, which are the
least studied.

00:03:03.439 --> 00:03:09.799
Then there are the Dictyostelids, also known
as cellular slime molds, and then there’s

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:16.760
the Myxomycetes, also known as the "true"
or "plasmodial" slime molds.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.930
Scientists love studying slime molds, particularly
Dictyostelids and Myxomycetes, for reasons

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:24.980
that we will discuss soon.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:31.030
In fact, the slime molds we're going to show
you today come from a lab, though we are on

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.510
the hunt to see if we can gather any of our
own in our sampling trips.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:43.080
This species is Physarum polycephalum, a well-studied example of Myxomycetes, making it a

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.540
plasmodial slime mold.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.689
We feed it oatmeal and barley flakes, keeping
it in the dark because it's not particularly

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:51.959
fond of light.

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:56.999
We're observing our Physarum here on petri
dishes because it's hard to transfer them

00:03:56.999 --> 00:03:58.420
to glass slides.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:04.159
This, in turn, limits our magnification, so
we won’t be able to see as deeply into their

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:08.540
bodies as we might be able to with other organisms.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:16.020
But they are remarkable to observe as an entity,
with their sprawling, networked branches.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.019
Now, we also don't have samples of the other
types of slime molds right now, so we're going

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:27.069
to be focusing our discussion mostly on Physarum
and plasmodial slime molds.

00:04:27.069 --> 00:04:33.789
But both cellular and plasmodial slime molds
come up often in the news because scientists

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:39.590
have observed them doing very impressive things,
but let’s start out by quickly explaining

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.460
the differences here.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:47.340
Cellular slime molds, the dictyostelids, are
made up of organisms that will likely spend

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:51.960
most of their lives as singular amoebas.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:05:00.009
But if the conditions around them become bleak,
these amoebas team up to create a multicellular

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:07.370
thing called a "slug" that coordinates the
various cells so that they can all move to

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:14.669
a good spot and then morph into a fruiting
body that releases spores.

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:16.280
This is wild, right?

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:21.460
They’re single cells and then they get some
signal that they should all come together

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:27.080
to form an organism and spore out to create
the next generation.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:34.050
But while cellular slime molds are made up
of many cells, true slime molds like Physarum

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:40.460
polycephalum are actually made up of only
one cell.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:48.410
Yeah, all of that branching weirdness, that
is all one cell.

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:52.700
The life of this Physarum started with the
sporangia,

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:56.889
a black globular body made by another Physarum.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:02.500
The sporangia holds spores that spread and
eventually germinate into either an amoeba

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.500
or a flagellate.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:10.659
At this unicellular stage in their lives,
as you might expect, the Physarum only has

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:12.110
one nucleus.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:16.360
But then the amoeba finds another amoeba to
mate with.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:23.550
And if you've heard about a slime mold that
has several hundred sexes, this is where that comes in.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:30.949
Each of the little cells produced by the spores
has two copies of three sex genes, and each

00:06:30.949 --> 00:06:35.879
of those sex genes has their own variants
across the species.

00:06:35.879 --> 00:06:40.919
Taking into account the number of different
combinations of those sex gene variants that

00:06:40.919 --> 00:06:48.610
are possible, you end up with an organism
whose sex is just one of hundreds, which increases

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:53.759
the number of possible mating partners for
any individual physarum amoeba.

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:58.930
When they do find their mate, the two become
one, literally.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:03.569
They fuse together all the way down to their
nucleus.

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:08.389
And after this point, the new organism doesn’t
divide anymore.

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:09.770
It grows.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:18.360
The plasmodium formed by the merged Physarum
cells expands, sometimes up to two feet in

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.430
diameter if the conditions are really good.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:27.889
But, the nuclei inside will continue dividing
and dividing and dividing, their count ending

00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:31.040
up in the millions.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:36.720
Physarum have actin and myosin, or muscle
proteins, the same ones we have, that act

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:43.759
as a part of a network to contract and relax
the cytoplasm, creating that stream you see

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:46.979
moving through the body of the organism.

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:55.360
This process moves the slime mold, which can
reach a speed of around 4 centimeters/hour,

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:03.979
and it also helps distribute nutrients around that ever-widening, increasingly gigantic body.

00:08:03.979 --> 00:08:10.229
And here's the thing: whatever it is these
amoebas have come together to create, it is

00:08:10.229 --> 00:08:13.699
very, very smart.

00:08:13.699 --> 00:08:19.289
Listing off the physarum talents that have
been unearthed by scientists is enough to

00:08:19.289 --> 00:08:23.180
inspire a sense of inadequacy.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:29.610
They can learn to manage uncomfortable stimuli
and pass on their knowledge to other Physarums

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.430
by fusing with them.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:38.270
They can also solve mazes and make good nutritional
choices, which, like, I can’t.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:46.960
In one of the most well-known Physarum experiments, scientists placed oat flakes down on an agar plate.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:53.570
These flakes were distributed in a pattern
similar to the arrangement of cities around Tokyo.

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:59.880
And then they watched as a Physarum that started
at the central Tokyo equivalent eventually

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:08.870
morphed itself into a route that resembled,
shockingly closely, the Tokyo rail system.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:16.670
The Physarum’s optimized hunt for food resembled
the efficiency that human engineers seek when

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.740
transporting people around cities.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:25.360
If you grew up watching Saturday morning cartoons
or even their blockbuster adaptations, you've

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.440
seen plenty of robots that assemble together
into some kind of megarobot.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:36.010
The bodies are metal and they’re full of
circuits, transforming the individual, fantastical

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:40.820
powers of those robots into a bigger stomping,
punching thing.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:48.650
Nature, as it inevitably seems to, got there
first, beating even our imaginations, and

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:55.830
with a touch that is so much lighter than
ours, yet still so incredible.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:10:02.820
Instead of imagined mechanical frames, it's
the organic bodies of amoeba assembling together

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:07.300
to create a bigger living, learning thing.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:11.230
The contours are smooth, the movements streaming.

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:20.510
The slime mold doesn't need to stomp or punch,
it just spreads and consumes and fruits, expanding

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:28.530
the microcosmos in its own body as it stakes
a claim in our world.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:35.130
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:39.760
Thank you to all of our new viewers who are
with us and thank you especially to all of

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.130
our patrons on Patreon, who make this show
possible.

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:48.360
If you’ve just stumbled across Journey to
the Microcosmos, these people on the screen

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:51.210
right here are the ones you really have to
thank.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:55.480
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James Weiss, you can find him

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:58.440
at Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram, and if you want
to

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.120
see more from us, you can go to youtube.com/microcosmos

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.080
where we have a new episode up every week.

